1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,967
Hi — I'm Divij Pawar. This video is about what actually makes

2
00:00:02,967 --> 00:00:05,800
an AI "agentic," and where that label stops being true.

3
00:00:06,333 --> 00:00:09,600
You've seen the word on every startup's landing page, every keynote slide,

4
00:00:09,867 --> 00:00:12,333
every LinkedIn post promising the future just showed up.

5
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:15,933
But here's the problem — half the products calling themselves "agentic" are

6
00:00:15,933 --> 00:00:18,233
doing something a form on a website could basically do.

7
00:00:18,633 --> 00:00:20,833
And the other half are running loops that can rack up real

8
00:00:20,833 --> 00:00:24,633
costs, or real consequences, while you're not even watching.

9
00:00:25,410 --> 00:00:26,877
So today, we're doing a teardown.

10
00:00:27,243 --> 00:00:30,577
Not a dictionary definition — a spectrum. We're taking one task, "book

11
00:00:30,577 --> 00:00:33,677
me a flight," and running it through four tiers of AI capability.

12
00:00:34,310 --> 00:00:36,943
By the end, you'll be able to look at any "agentic" claim,

13
00:00:36,943 --> 00:00:39,077
and know exactly where it actually lives.

14
00:00:39,810 --> 00:00:42,410
Let's start at the floor. Tier zero is a single question, a

15
00:00:42,410 --> 00:00:45,443
single answer. You type: "book me a flight to Chicago next Friday."

16
00:00:46,010 --> 00:00:49,043
The AI replies with words — maybe something like, "Try Google Flights,

17
00:00:49,210 --> 00:00:51,543
compare a few times, book whichever fits your schedule."

18
00:00:51,977 --> 00:00:55,510
Helpful advice. Zero action. Here's what's actually happening under the hood. The

19
00:00:55,510 --> 00:00:58,510
model is predicting the next likely word, over and over, until it's

20
00:00:58,510 --> 00:00:59,410
built a full sentence.

21
00:00:59,843 --> 00:01:03,810
That's the entire mechanism. There is no flight search, no browser, no

22
00:01:03,810 --> 00:01:06,777
bank card. If this were a person, it's like texting your

23
00:01:07,143 --> 00:01:10,410
well-traveled friend for advice. Great advice — but they didn't lift a

24
00:01:10,410 --> 00:01:11,443
finger to book anything.

25
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:13,753
Tier one is where things get real.

26
00:01:14,153 --> 00:01:16,253
Now the model has been given access to a tool — say,

27
00:01:16,387 --> 00:01:17,487
a flight search function.

28
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:20,487
You ask the exact same question, and this time it doesn't just

29
00:01:20,487 --> 00:01:23,287
talk about flights. It sends a request to a real flight search

30
00:01:23,287 --> 00:01:26,887
system, gets back live prices and times, and shows you actual options.

31
00:01:27,253 --> 00:01:30,253
This is what people mean by "function calling," or "tool use."

32
00:01:30,753 --> 00:01:33,187
The model recognized it needed outside information,

33
00:01:33,753 --> 00:01:36,953
formatted a request the tool could understand, waited for the result, and

34
00:01:36,953 --> 00:01:39,887
used it. But watch closely — it stops there. It hands you

35
00:01:39,887 --> 00:01:42,687
a list. You still pick a flight, enter your card, hit purchase.

36
00:01:43,253 --> 00:01:46,653
One tool call, one job done, then control comes straight back to

37
00:01:46,653 --> 00:01:47,453
you.

38
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:50,160
Tier two is where the AI stops acting like a search bar,

39
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:52,293
and starts acting more like an actual assistant.

40
00:01:52,793 --> 00:01:55,827
Instead of one tool call, it chains several together, building the plan

41
00:01:55,827 --> 00:01:56,660
itself as it goes.

42
00:01:57,193 --> 00:01:59,560
Say it differently this time: "book me a flight to Chicago next

43
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:02,293
Friday, but only if it doesn't clash with anything on my calendar."

44
00:02:02,927 --> 00:02:05,993
Now the model has to actually check your calendar, search flights, line

45
00:02:05,993 --> 00:02:08,160
the two up against each other, notice you've got a

46
00:02:08,593 --> 00:02:11,727
nine a.m. meeting that same morning, and come back with something like

47
00:02:11,860 --> 00:02:14,493
— "There's a seven a.m. flight that works, or an eleven a.m.

48
00:02:14,493 --> 00:02:16,793
one that would make you miss your meeting. Which do you want?"

49
00:02:16,993 --> 00:02:20,427
That's planning. A fuzzy goal got broken into ordered steps, multiple tools

50
00:02:20,427 --> 00:02:22,993
got used in sequence, and the plan adjusted based on what it

51
00:02:22,993 --> 00:02:24,293
actually found along the way.

52
00:02:24,827 --> 00:02:28,493
This is the tier most products marketed as "AI agents" genuinely live

53
00:02:28,493 --> 00:02:32,327
in. It looks autonomous. But it's bounded — a handful of steps,

54
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:34,993
then it checks back in with you, before spending your money.

55
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:38,373
And now, tier three — the one everybody actually means when they

56
00:02:38,373 --> 00:02:41,773
say "agentic," even though most products on the market can't really do

57
00:02:41,773 --> 00:02:42,573
it yet.

58
00:02:42,573 --> 00:02:44,873
This is an AI that doesn't just run a plan once and

59
00:02:44,873 --> 00:02:45,673
stop.

60
00:02:45,607 --> 00:02:48,807
It runs in an ongoing loop — watching, deciding, acting, remembering —

61
00:02:48,807 --> 00:02:51,873
and it keeps working toward a goal over hours, days, sometimes weeks,

62
00:02:52,173 --> 00:02:54,207
without you standing over its shoulder at every step.

63
00:02:54,607 --> 00:02:55,507
Picture this instead.

64
00:02:56,173 --> 00:02:58,507
You say it once: "get me the best fare to Chicago sometime

65
00:02:58,507 --> 00:03:00,707
this month, book it the moment the price looks good."

66
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:04,307
Tier three means it checks prices every day, on its own. It

67
00:03:04,307 --> 00:03:07,240
remembers that you like aisle seats and morning departures, because you mentioned

68
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:09,773
that in a completely different conversation three weeks ago.

69
00:03:10,173 --> 00:03:13,473
It notices the airline cancelled your original flight, rebooks you automatically,

70
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:17,607
and only messages you afterward to say, "handled it." Nothing restarted from

71
00:03:17,607 --> 00:03:18,407
zero.

72
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:22,507
It's carrying context and decisions forward across time, and making its own

73
00:03:22,507 --> 00:03:26,573
calls about when to act. This is genuinely powerful — and genuinely

74
00:03:26,573 --> 00:03:27,373
risky.

75
00:03:27,710 --> 00:03:28,977
So here's your teardown checklist.

76
00:03:29,343 --> 00:03:32,443
Next time you see "agentic AI" slapped on a landing page, ask

77
00:03:32,443 --> 00:03:35,243
three questions. One — does it use tools, or does it just

78
00:03:35,243 --> 00:03:37,910
talk? If it's just talk, it's tier zero, no matter how it's

79
00:03:37,910 --> 00:03:38,710
branded.

80
00:03:38,643 --> 00:03:40,843
Two — does it chain multiple steps into a plan, or does

81
00:03:40,843 --> 00:03:43,610
it fire off one tool call and stop? Chaining is tier two

82
00:03:43,610 --> 00:03:44,410
behavior.

83
00:03:44,477 --> 00:03:46,543
Three — does it keep running without you, and does it remember

84
00:03:46,543 --> 00:03:47,877
what happened in past sessions?

85
00:03:48,477 --> 00:03:51,777
That's the only real tier three. Most of what's sold as "agentic"

86
00:03:51,810 --> 00:03:54,443
today comfortably sits in tier one, or tier two.

87
00:03:54,910 --> 00:03:56,410
True tier three — autonomous,

88
00:03:56,777 --> 00:04:00,310
memory-carrying loops — is rare, harder to control, and honestly still being

89
00:04:00,310 --> 00:04:02,643
figured out by the best labs building this stuff.

90
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:06,353
So here's the whole spectrum in one frame. Tier zero talks and

91
00:04:06,353 --> 00:04:09,853
touches nothing. Tier one makes a single tool call and hands control

92
00:04:09,853 --> 00:04:13,487
straight back. Tier two plans several steps, adapts, and still checks in

93
00:04:13,487 --> 00:04:16,487
before it spends anything. Tier three runs a loop with memory, for

94
00:04:16,487 --> 00:04:17,387
days, without you.

95
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:21,087
"Agentic" was never a yes-or-no label. It's a spectrum — of how

96
00:04:21,087 --> 00:04:23,453
much a system does on its own, and how far it carries

97
00:04:23,453 --> 00:04:24,620
what it knows between actions.

98
00:04:25,353 --> 00:04:27,187
Now you know exactly where to look.

99
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:31,007
Your turn. Here's the prompt: "Audit an AI product I use. Which

100
00:04:31,107 --> 00:04:33,140
tier is it really — zero, one, two, or three — and

101
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:34,173
what evidence proves it?"

102
00:04:34,673 --> 00:04:37,007
Run that on a tool you actually pay for. Then check the

103
00:04:37,007 --> 00:04:39,873
answer against three things. Does it name the specific tools the product

104
00:04:39,873 --> 00:04:42,373
can call? Does it show a plan running more than one step?

105
00:04:42,673 --> 00:04:44,807
And does it prove memory carries across separate sessions?

106
00:04:45,207 --> 00:04:47,673
If a tier three claim comes back without that last one, the

107
00:04:47,673 --> 00:04:48,473
claim is marketing.

108
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:49,900
Signing off — Divij Pawar.
